    ('8426a941-5472-44b1-b7aa-0872fa5ae26d'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Granite circle 44.3 m across on Bodmin Moor, dated 4300–3300 BC, enclosed within a 53 m ditch-and-bank platform. A fallen menhir 3.7 m long lies at its centre.', 'On Bodmin Moor a ditch and bank ring the Stripple Stones, four granite stones still standing among fallen ones, set only approximately 1.5 feet deep, the entrance facing the Trippet stones.', 'teaser-card-s009:8426a941-5472-44b1-b7aa-0872fa5ae26d:card_description', 'teaser-card-s009 (teaser-card): teaser card, 189 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '5d3df280746f809f4ac3e50de2d548bfca5384d80e5ea301e148767ef19b5bda|880e1c98baedaa3c5fd079fa6327f058c90ea8d204896d0d518a2c7984d68dd1|880e1c98baedaa3c5fd079fa6327f058c90ea8d204896d0d518a2c7984d68dd1', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-write-016", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On Bodmin Moor a ditch and bank ring the Stripple Stones, four granite stones still standing among fallen ones, set only approximately 1.5 feet deep, the entrance facing the Trippet stones."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-check-016 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the Stripple Stones are on Bodmin Moor [S1]; a ditch and a bank (vallum) ring the circle [S3]; four granite stones still stand [S4]; several stones have fallen [S4]; the stones were set only approximately 1.5 feet deep [S7]; the entrance faces the Trippet stones [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-verify-048 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Stripple Stones are a henge and stone circle (a ring monument). [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stripple_stones]; The Stripple Stones lie on Bodmin Moor. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stripple_stones]; A ditch and bank ring the circle. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stripple_stones]; Four granite stones still stand, among fallen ones. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stripple_stones]; The stones were set only approximately 1.5 feet deep. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stripple_stones]; The entrance faces the Trippet stones. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stripple_stones]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('8484a13c-bf95-4bbd-bb4b-62d5033a4db8'::uuid, 'card_description', 'A Pompeii house decorated entirely in the Fourth Style from a single period -- a rarity, since most houses mix styles from different centuries.', 'In 1898 the Prince and Princess of Naples attended a dig in Pompeii''s House of the Prince of Naples, a home painted throughout in a single style, its rich rooms set around a central garden.', 'teaser-card-s009:8484a13c-bf95-4bbd-bb4b-62d5033a4db8:card_description', 'teaser-card-s009 (teaser-card): teaser card, 189 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', 'be90b9c43f296c14c7afde454b50ae57b6c04a51e6a6a7b017da5daf71a566a4|9f7e571ed2a1fc5f7b24594dd4425ed4213d539913e14361f7184a28d59063ec|9f7e571ed2a1fc5f7b24594dd4425ed4213d539913e14361f7184a28d59063ec', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-write-016", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "In 1898 the Prince and Princess of Naples attended a dig in Pompeii''s House of the Prince of Naples, a home painted throughout in a single style, its rich rooms set around a central garden."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-check-016 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the Prince and Princess of Naples attended an excavation of the house [S2]; this happened in 1898 [S2]; the house is in Pompeii [S1]; the house is a home (a Roman domus) [S1]; it is painted throughout in a single style [S3]; it has richly decorated rooms around a central garden [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-verify-048 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The House of the Prince of Naples is a home (a Roman domus) in Pompeii. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/House_of_the_Prince_of_Naples]; The Prince and Princess of Naples attended a dig (a ceremonial excavation) in the house. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/House_of_the_Prince_of_Naples]; The dig the Prince and Princess attended took place in 1898. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/House_of_the_Prince_of_Naples]; The house is painted throughout. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/House_of_the_Prince_of_Naples]; The painting throughout is in a single style. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/House_of_the_Prince_of_Naples]; The house has rich (richly decorated) rooms. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/House_of_the_Prince_of_Naples]; The rooms are set around a central garden. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/House_of_the_Prince_of_Naples]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('84894867-d7b1-49b7-a1e7-398e73a725f2'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Four Neo-Assyrian bas-reliefs from 704–681 BC carved into a cliff face, each showing a procession of nine figures — the king before the great gods of Assyria.', 'Near Duhok, Halamata Cave bears Assyrian reliefs of nine figures in procession, first photographed in 1909 and linked to the canals King Sennacherib built to carry water to Nineveh.', 'teaser-card-s009:84894867-d7b1-49b7-a1e7-398e73a725f2:card_description', 'teaser-card-s009 (teaser-card): teaser card, 181 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', 'bb4bb11db624eec0304cf3001a4fd770fa8b468cab31eb4442eccab977db30ed|330bc3947446ebfeb16d52f8fa2c91cd0b5a8bbb1b9a488f5876c2a2140d119a|330bc3947446ebfeb16d52f8fa2c91cd0b5a8bbb1b9a488f5876c2a2140d119a', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-write-016", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Near Duhok, Halamata Cave bears Assyrian reliefs of nine figures in procession, first photographed in 1909 and linked to the canals King Sennacherib built to carry water to Nineveh."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-check-016 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Halamata Cave is near Duhok [S1]; the cave bears Assyrian reliefs [S2]; the reliefs show a procession of nine figures [S5]; the reliefs were first photographed in 1909 [S5]; the reliefs are linked to the canal system built by King Sennacherib [S7]; the canals carried water to Nineveh [S7]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-verify-048 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Halamata Cave is an archaeological site near Duhok (Iraq). [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Halamata_Cave]; The cave bears Assyrian reliefs. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Halamata_Cave]; The reliefs show nine figures. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Halamata_Cave]; The nine figures are in procession. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Halamata_Cave]; The reliefs were first photographed in 1909. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Halamata_Cave]; The reliefs are linked to the canals King Sennacherib built. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Halamata_Cave]; Sennacherib built the canals to carry water to Nineveh. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Halamata_Cave]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('84b456ff-0b44-4d75-92a4-5cbe22026b95'::uuid, 'card_description', 'A 40 km wide geological dome in the Sahara exposing concentric rings of rock. It is visible from orbit and was once proposed as Plato''s Atlantis.', 'Called the Eye of Africa, the Richat Structure is a deeply eroded Sahara dome 40 kilometres wide, with exceptional accumulations of Acheulean artifacts along the wadis of its outer ring.', 'teaser-card-s009:84b456ff-0b44-4d75-92a4-5cbe22026b95:card_description', 'teaser-card-s009 (teaser-card): teaser card, 186 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', 'ce87ddd369acb299aa8442f9e1e4b9c716d6e278eb5cd77387ffb00e206d8dbb|b5cd37461bc9a60a67dc2168c27cf1f22fb580d15c2e817b1c671611d5401e16|b5cd37461bc9a60a67dc2168c27cf1f22fb580d15c2e817b1c671611d5401e16', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-write-016", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Called the Eye of Africa, the Richat Structure is a deeply eroded Sahara dome 40 kilometres wide, with exceptional accumulations of Acheulean artifacts along the wadis of its outer ring."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-check-016 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the Richat Structure is called the Eye of Africa [S1]; it is a deeply eroded dome [S3]; it lies in the Sahara [S1]; it is 40 kilometres wide [S3]; it holds exceptional accumulations of Acheulean artifacts [S5]; the Acheulean sites lie along wadis in its outermost ring [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-verify-048 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Richat Structure is a deeply eroded dome in the Sahara (Mauritania). [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Richat_Structure]; The structure lies in the Sahara. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Richat_Structure]; It is called the Eye of Africa. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Richat_Structure]; The dome is 40 kilometres wide. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Richat_Structure]; It holds exceptional accumulations of Acheulean artifacts. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Richat_Structure]; The Acheulean sites lie along the wadis of its outer ring. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Richat_Structure]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('84c1ff70-a242-423d-96f8-694b1b7fcc67'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Gazma Cave is a Paleolithic dwelling site discovered between Ordubad and Sharur districts in 1983.', 'Gazma Cave, a Paleolithic dwelling site 1450 m above sea level, revealed 6 cultural layers, fauna fossils and 874 stone tools, similar in form to those from Azokh and Tağlar caves.', 'teaser-card-s009:84c1ff70-a242-423d-96f8-694b1b7fcc67:card_description', 'teaser-card-s009 (teaser-card): teaser card, 180 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '6a79b663baf4247b19c9aa74608000f6f43ebdfb16b3c26a0ac00ab32a0caea7|f8dd9225240ced2f2c7c20fb1ae8ce23a0aff5179f835a0f5569522b6cf6222d|f8dd9225240ced2f2c7c20fb1ae8ce23a0aff5179f835a0f5569522b6cf6222d', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-rewrite1-001", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Gazma Cave, a Paleolithic dwelling site 1450 m above sea level, revealed 6 cultural layers, fauna fossils and 874 stone tools, similar in form to those from Azokh and Tağlar caves."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-check1-001 (check1), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Gazma Cave is a Paleolithic dwelling site [S1]; it lies 1450 m above sea level [S2]; 6 cultural layers were discovered [S6]; fauna fossils were found [S4]; 874 stone tools were found [S4]; the tools are similar in form to those from Azokh and Tağlar caves [S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-verify-048 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Gazma Cave is a Paleolithic dwelling site. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gazma_Cave]; The cave lies 1450 m above sea level. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gazma_Cave]; The excavation revealed 6 cultural layers. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gazma_Cave]; The cave yielded fauna fossils. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gazma_Cave]; The cave yielded 874 stone tools. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gazma_Cave]; The tools are similar in form to those from Azokh and Tağlar caves. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gazma_Cave]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('85652e72-e8d5-4c37-8c60-f5d9fd0b1edd'::uuid, 'card_description', 'A Roman temple built over a Greek or Samnite capitolium, later converted into a cathedral. Layers of pagan and Christian worship stack on top of each other.', 'Nine fluted Corinthian columns flank each long side of the Temple of Augustus, its cella of white marble blocks, all built without mortar, later rededicated as a church to Proculus.', 'teaser-card-s009:85652e72-e8d5-4c37-8c60-f5d9fd0b1edd:card_description', 'teaser-card-s009 (teaser-card): teaser card, 181 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '66ec8f4b96c5f2fd907d7de657e06c0684297f621ace6d332d8b02331a58354b|e05701ca4aeb0156da61036f544a7847d07e6421b3921b636cedff919ed57b2a|e05701ca4aeb0156da61036f544a7847d07e6421b3921b636cedff919ed57b2a', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-write-017", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Nine fluted Corinthian columns flank each long side of the Temple of Augustus, its cella of white marble blocks, all built without mortar, later rededicated as a church to Proculus."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-check-017 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the temple has nine fluted Corinthian columns [S1]; the columns flank each long side of the temple [S1]; the cella is of white marble blocks [S1]; the whole temple was built without mortar [S2]; the temple was later rededicated as a church [S3]; the church was dedicated to Proculus [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-verify-049 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Temple of Augustus is a Roman temple, the former temple that housed the church (now Pozzuoli Cathedral) at the Rione Terra in Pozzuoli [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pozzuoli_Cathedral]; Pozzuoli, where the temple stands, is in Italy [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pozzuoli]; Nine fluted Corinthian columns flank each long side of the temple [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pozzuoli_Cathedral]; The cella is of white marble blocks [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pozzuoli_Cathedral]; The whole temple was built without mortar [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pozzuoli_Cathedral]; The temple was later rededicated as a church to Proculus [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pozzuoli_Cathedral]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('8590c435-e895-4d7e-9b06-d0f2027c04d7'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Burial towers and archaeological remains on the high Altiplano, declared a National Historical Monument in 2008. Adjacent chullpa towers mark a pre-Inca funerary landscape of the Oruro highlands.', 'Burial towers stand beside Inka Murata, an archaeological site on the Altiplano of Bolivia in the community of Escara, together declared a National Historical Monument on January 23, 2008.', 'teaser-card-s009:8590c435-e895-4d7e-9b06-d0f2027c04d7:card_description', 'teaser-card-s009 (teaser-card): teaser card, 188 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', 'cd595a532f6537aae989c4c899de070aed059ce70636ea22a12272f13567a09a|d191a5ee9679699dc7d3716bf2276b7b59caea47eda4936f393a5f3db65635bf|d191a5ee9679699dc7d3716bf2276b7b59caea47eda4936f393a5f3db65635bf', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-write-017", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Burial towers stand beside Inka Murata, an archaeological site on the Altiplano of Bolivia in the community of Escara, together declared a National Historical Monument on January 23, 2008."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-check-017 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "burial towers stand beside Inka Murata [S3]; Inka Murata is an archaeological site [S1]; the site lies on the Altiplano of Bolivia [S1]; the site is in the community of Escara [S2]; the site and the burial towers together were declared a National Historical Monument [S3]; the declaration was on January 23, 2008 [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-verify-049 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Inka Murata is an archaeological site in Bolivia [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inka_Murata]; The site lies on the Altiplano of Bolivia [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inka_Murata]; The site is in the community of Escara [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inka_Murata]; Burial towers (chullpas) stand adjacent to the Inka Murata site [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inka_Murata]; Site and burial towers together were declared a National Historical Monument by law [SUPPORTED: https://www.lexivox.org/norms/BO-L-3833.xhtml]; The declaration was on January 23, 2008 (Law 3833) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inka_Murata]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('861015a5-d704-4231-a835-069e4ee5b4a4'::uuid, 'card_description', 'The Stanegate is believed to have been built under the governorship of Agricola, from 77 to 85 AD, during the reigns of the emperors Vespasian, Titus, and Domitian.', 'Several decades before Hadrian''s Wall, the Stanegate, the stone road, carried a Roman frontier through the valleys of the Tyne and Irthing, linking forts from Corbridge to Carlisle.', 'teaser-card-s009:861015a5-d704-4231-a835-069e4ee5b4a4:card_description', 'teaser-card-s009 (teaser-card): teaser card, 181 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '2ba71a5a7bedbaa974fc19e01308c038bdba9a8706be5b37c0f06d7df885068e|f8676e33decb2eab01010e5599251d9cd7d264721f31216edeee4a78ac14c8c9|f8676e33decb2eab01010e5599251d9cd7d264721f31216edeee4a78ac14c8c9', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-write-017", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Several decades before Hadrian''s Wall, the Stanegate, the stone road, carried a Roman frontier through the valleys of the Tyne and Irthing, linking forts from Corbridge to Carlisle."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-check-017 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the Stanegate predates Hadrian''s Wall by several decades [S4]; the Stanegate means the stone road [S1]; the Stanegate was a Roman road and frontier [S1]; it ran through the valleys of the Tyne and Irthing [S3]; it linked forts from Corbridge to Carlisle [S2]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-verify-049 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Stanegate is a Roman road and early frontier in northern England [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stanegate]; Stanegate means ''stone road'' [SUPPORTED, no proving quote: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stanegate]; The Stanegate predated Hadrian''s Wall by several decades [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stanegate]; The Stanegate ran through the valleys of the Tyne and the Irthing [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stanegate]; The Stanegate linked forts from Corbridge to Carlisle [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stanegate]; The Stanegate carried a Roman frontier (it became the frontier line after the withdrawal from Scotland) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stanegate]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('86c101ad-fcdb-413a-8aec-6aad2d1574e7'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Dykyi Sad was a fortified urban settlement of the final Bronze Age, located in the historical center of modern-day Mykolaiv.', 'On a high promontory where the Southern Bug meets the Inhul, the Dykyi Sad Archaeological Site yielded over 50 architectural objects, including craft workshops and a sanctuary with a ramp.', 'teaser-card-s009:86c101ad-fcdb-413a-8aec-6aad2d1574e7:card_description', 'teaser-card-s009 (teaser-card): teaser card, 188 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '618aa031d8ea3166f0d68661be6beb3b91ed5b8cd9b16cffa80b63e7a2418b63|1407222f182704df6d6039812afa3abd3d03582e883c5f148fcdbc577fd3ec6c|1407222f182704df6d6039812afa3abd3d03582e883c5f148fcdbc577fd3ec6c', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-rewrite-v-002", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On a high promontory where the Southern Bug meets the Inhul, the Dykyi Sad Archaeological Site yielded over 50 architectural objects, including craft workshops and a sanctuary with a ramp."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-check-v-002 (check-v), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site lies on a high promontory [S2]; the promontory is where the Southern Bug meets the Inhul (their confluence) [S2]; over 50 architectural objects have been excavated [S7]; they include craft workshops [S7]; they include a sanctuary with a ramp [S7]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-verify2-005 (verify2), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Dykyi Sad is an archaeological site: a fortified settlement (proto-city) of the final Bronze Age in Mykolaiv, Ukraine [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dykyi_Sad_archaeological_site]; The site stands on a high promontory where the Southern Bug meets the Inhul [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dykyi_Sad_archaeological_site]; Over 50 architectural objects have been excavated [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dykyi_Sad_archaeological_site]; The architectural objects include craft workshops [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dykyi_Sad_archaeological_site]; The architectural objects include a sanctuary with a ramp [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dykyi_Sad_archaeological_site]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('875c0673-36e1-4296-a7af-29637effcec3'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Settlement began after 1130 AD; by 1350 the Mogollon culture was raising multi-storied adobe structures across this Chihuahuan desert site. Construction stopped abruptly around 1450 AD.', 'In Chihuahua, evidence indicates Paquime had underground drains, reservoirs, water channels to its homes and a sewage system. Burned about 1340, it was rebuilt with multi-story apartments.', 'teaser-card-s009:875c0673-36e1-4296-a7af-29637effcec3:card_description', 'teaser-card-s009 (teaser-card): teaser card, 188 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '89a3b83c4025c9000434c86462d35c420c5c5ebc21330a243bd0f6a872103ada|c5692f2e9b2794c75b41608da4fc3a87ce4043692f86ad58e933656ef3c3ce90|c5692f2e9b2794c75b41608da4fc3a87ce4043692f86ad58e933656ef3c3ce90', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-rewrite1-001", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "In Chihuahua, evidence indicates Paquime had underground drains, reservoirs, water channels to its homes and a sewage system. Burned about 1340, it was rebuilt with multi-story apartments."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-check1-001 (check1), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site is in Chihuahua [S1]; evidence indicates it had underground drains [S5]; evidence indicates it had reservoirs [S5]; evidence indicates it had water channels to the homes [S5]; evidence indicates it had a sewage system [S5]; it was burned about 1340 [S6]; it was rebuilt with multi-story apartments [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-verify-049 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Paquime is a prehistoric archaeological site (settlement) in the Mexican state of Chihuahua [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Casas_Grandes]; Evidence indicates Paquime had a water management system (the text''s framing that this rests on evidence) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Casas_Grandes]; Paquime had underground drains [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Casas_Grandes]; Paquime had reservoirs [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Casas_Grandes]; Paquime had water channels to its homes [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Casas_Grandes]; Paquime had a sewage system [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Casas_Grandes]; Paquime was burned about 1340 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Casas_Grandes]; After the burning it was rebuilt with multi-story apartments [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Casas_Grandes]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('8944e529-490d-499c-94d2-9c81cace802b'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Paeonian city from the 6th century BC near Knezhje. In 2008 the main gate was found intact -- confirming this as a major early urban centre and reshaping understanding of Paeonian civilization.', 'Bylazora, a Paeonian city of early classic antiquity, appears in Livy: in 168 BC Perseus asked a Gaulish army campaigning in Maedica to shift its camp to this place in Paeonia.', 'teaser-card-s009:8944e529-490d-499c-94d2-9c81cace802b:card_description', 'teaser-card-s009 (teaser-card): teaser card, 176 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '8119d280b6d29a2dd7aa435e523dd09aff2591a7ff568f1311469c857d67b997|6b715f23bde1c46c7ef908cf3e9212c01a0c13b47cef8765fd6ee40b72a73ccf|6b715f23bde1c46c7ef908cf3e9212c01a0c13b47cef8765fd6ee40b72a73ccf', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-write-017", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Bylazora, a Paeonian city of early classic antiquity, appears in Livy: in 168 BC Perseus asked a Gaulish army campaigning in Maedica to shift its camp to this place in Paeonia."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-check-017 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Bylazora was a Paeonian city [S1]; it dates from the period of early classic antiquity [S1]; Bylazora is mentioned by Livy [S2]; in 168 BC Perseus asked a Gaulish army to shift its camp to Bylazora [S2]; the Gaulish army was campaigning in Maedica [S2]; Bylazora is a place in Paeonia [S2]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-verify-050 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Bylazora is an ancient city (a city, in North Macedonia near Sveti Nikole) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bylazora]; Bylazora was a Paeonian city [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bylazora]; Bylazora dates to early classic antiquity [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bylazora]; Bylazora appears (is mentioned) in Livy [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bylazora]; In 168 BC Perseus asked a Gaulish army to shift its camp to Bylazora [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bylazora]; The Gaulish army was campaigning in Maedica [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bylazora]; Bylazora is a place in Paeonia [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bylazora]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('89bdbe2c-a19b-484d-b6ef-2ed464b1311b'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Maya ruins largely unexcavated in the Macal River catchment, positioned as a satellite site of Xunantunich. What lies beneath this jungle floor remains mostly untouched.', 'Near Chaa Creek, a tributary of the Macal River in western Belize, lies an ancient Maya settlement, posited to be a satellite of Xunantunich, where significant pottery was recovered.', 'teaser-card-s009:89bdbe2c-a19b-484d-b6ef-2ed464b1311b:card_description', 'teaser-card-s009 (teaser-card): teaser card, 182 characters, checked PASS, web-verified VERIFIED', '3c75209a49e4e3e602f1cef2a4b269635d92debf0129d9d552a38ae89fb1cd31|b16ce1eaeabc9b5eba08910f31c3bd17c1f485bab0e0bcf3c015e2735eef973e|b16ce1eaeabc9b5eba08910f31c3bd17c1f485bab0e0bcf3c015e2735eef973e', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-write-017", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Near Chaa Creek, a tributary of the Macal River in western Belize, lies an ancient Maya settlement, posited to be a satellite of Xunantunich, where significant pottery was recovered."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-check-017 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Chaa Creek is a tributary of the Macal River [S1]; it is in western Belize [S1]; an ancient Maya settlement lies near Chaa Creek [S2]; the site is posited to be a satellite of Xunantunich [S5]; significant pottery was recovered there [S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-04: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-04-verify-050 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-04/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Chaa Creek is near the site of an ancient Maya settlement (the central claim: a Maya settlement near Chaa Creek) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chaa_Creek]; Chaa Creek is a tributary of the